<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Analytics (C061, analytics-read-model) — the read model behind the Admin Portal's dashboard
    statistics filter (AL-38, D-38, US-24.7, SCR-AP-002).

    Three things. A **materialisation job** that recomputes `analytics.daily_metrics` — one row per
    Asia/Colombo day carrying completed trips, gross fare, new riders, new drivers and daily-fee
    revenue. A **period aggregation** over those rows (today | week | month | custom) with the
    vs-previous-period deltas the contract defines. And the **live block** — online drivers, pending
    verifications, open tickets — which is read from the operational tables at request time and
    never from a rollup, because a rolled-up "currently online" is wrong by construction.

    FENCES:
      * **Derived, never authoritative.** The only table this assembly writes is
        `analytics.daily_metrics`. Every other statement here is a SELECT, and
        `TheOnlyTableThisComponentWritesIsAnalyticsDailyMetrics` in Analytics.Tests reflects over
        every SQL constant in the assembly and proves it rather than trusting a reviewer.
      * **Asia/Colombo decides the day, not UTC (D-38).** Every boundary comes from
        `MageRide.Shared.Time.BusinessCalendar`; there is no `::date` cast on a UTC timestamp
        anywhere in this component.

    Microsoft.NET.Sdk, NOT Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web — and this is the one structural decision of the
    component. This is a **library referenced by admin-bff (C062)**, not a service on a socket:

      * D3' gives the surface to admin-bff — `GET /v1/admin/dashboard/stats` and `stats.csv` are
        operations of `backend/contracts/admin-bff.yaml`, and D6' §I-28.5 says that endpoint
        "aggregates `analytics.daily_metrics`" rather than calling anything;
      * ADD §6's service table has no `analytics-svc` row (only AL-38's affected-areas column
        mentions the name), and the build planner recorded `analytics-read-model` among the
        components with **no `.yaml`** because "none has an HTTP contract in D3'"
        (build/progress.md, C007 finding (j));
      * an HTTP hop would put a network boundary between admin-bff and a read model that lives in
        the same database it already holds a connection to.

    So C062 calls `AddMageRideAnalytics(configuration)` and maps its own contracted routes onto
    `IDashboardStatsService` / `IAnalyticsRollupService`. Nothing here draws a route, and nothing
    here needs a container of its own.

    Verify: dotnet test backend/src/Analytics.Tests -c Release
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>MageRide.Analytics</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MageRide.Analytics</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The rollup is a BackgroundService and the options bind through IOptions; both live in the
         ASP.NET Core shared framework rather than in packages, exactly as MageRide.Shared takes
         them. There is no HTTP surface here — see the header. -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Dapper, Npgsql, the connection factory, the Colombo business calendar and the RFC 7807
         error registry all arrive through the kernel. This component adds no package of its own:
         the CSV is written here and the rollup is five aggregate queries. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MageRide.Shared\MageRide.Shared.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Analytics.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
